  "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/40fd26817dc71b5ceee1861e20a812a1835fdb92d0ff7a903d05aacc2eca2c05.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(9)(5)+2(12)(-3) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 18 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
